{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793343340,"nonce":"MAxRp3I3WHBqDCP5xr98dNPYQHs=","title":"Separate hosted recovery records by compute-confirmed document identity","message":"Parent: 9d6a14bfc40bc5c2eb5a105609e1c2a1c37f3766d131dae8f4da586edd8b7b3c\nNative foundation: ea9e535\n\nThe native stable-ID work exposed a path-reuse gap in the Durable Object journal: after deletion and recreation, the new CRDT correctly lacks its predecessor's history, but the gateway still asks it to acknowledge the predecessor's durable vector. Binding all records only to path/source would also merge distinct saved file histories.\n\nTrack successful native opens and closes in bounded per-connection metadata; derive Update/Save bindings from compute replies and verify those IDs back at compute. Key new journal/checkpoint records by path, source generation and document ID. Preserve older records for export and retain existing storage quotas. Legacy records may be bound in place only after native journal/checkpoint proofs confirm previously acknowledged history. Historical checkpoint imports must identify a bound record explicitly; unbound imports cannot select another document's head.\n\nValidate legacy adoption without graph copying, old snapshot/deletion protection, stale-ID refusal, deletion/recreation with distinct durable records, old record export after cloud restart, missing-history refusal and existing quota/authentication tests. Keep public hosted document moves and automatic restoration gated until browser and cross-host identity coordination is complete.","files":null}]}